#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

/*
We have N souvenir shops, each with price p[i].
At each shop, the buyer pays with 1000-yen bills only.
If p[i] < 1000, they receive one 500-yen coin iff the remainder includes 500,
i.e. iff 500 <= 1000 - p[i] <= 999, equivalently p[i] <= 500.
More generally, the classic interpretation of this problem is:

- Start with some amount of money.
- For each item, you must pay at least its price.
- We want to maximize the number of 500-yen coins in change.
- Among all such ways, minimize total amount paid.

This is equivalent to:
For each price p, choose a payment amount x which is a multiple of 1000 plus current coins,
but the standard DP solution for the known problem reduces to:
maximize count of items for which paid amount - price yields at least one 500 coin,
while minimizing total payment.

A standard and correct formulation:
For each item of price p, choose to pay either:
- exactly ceil(p/1000)*1000  -> no special 500 gain
- or ceil((p+500)/1000)*1000 -> gain one 500 coin
The extra cost to gain one 500 coin is:
((1000 - p % 1000) % 1000) adjusted relative to crossing the 500 boundary.
However, the original AOJ-style problem this statement matches is solved by DP on remainder modulo 1000.

State:
dp[r] = best pair (max_500coins, min_total_payment) after processing some items,
where r is current amount of money modulo 1000 carried forward.
Transition by choosing how many 1000-yen bills to use for current price.

But there is a much simpler equivalent DP:
Let total paid be S, total prices sum to P. Change is S-P.
Number of 500-yen coins obtainable from total change depends on how change is split per shop,
so we must process each shop independently.

For one shop with price p and current 500-yen coin availability represented by remainder r:
If we have r yen (<1000) in smaller money before the shop, then to pay price p we need
k thousand-yen bills where k is minimum such that r + 1000*k >= p.
New remainder becomes r + 1000*k - p.
We gain one 500-yen coin iff new remainder >= 500.
Among choices, using more than minimum k only increases payment by 1000 and doesn't improve
future state beyond same remainder, so only minimum k matters.

Thus DP over remainder 0..999:
start dp[0] = (0,0)
transition deterministically for each price.

Tie-break: maximize coins, then minimize expense.
*/

struct State {
    int coins;
    long long pay;
    bool ok;
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int N;
    while (cin >> N) {
        if (N == 0) break;
        vector<int> p(N);
        for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) cin >> p[i];

        const long long INF = (1LL << 60);
        vector<State> dp(1000, {-1, INF, false}), ndp(1000, {-1, INF, false});
        dp[0] = {0, 0, true};

        for (int price : p) {
            for (int r = 0; r < 1000; ++r) ndp[r] = {-1, INF, false};

            for (int r = 0; r < 1000; ++r) {
                if (!dp[r].ok) continue;

                int need = max(0, price - r);
                int k = (need + 999) / 1000;      // minimum number of 1000-yen bills
                int nr = r + 1000 * k - price;    // new remainder, 0..999
                int add = (nr >= 500) ? 1 : 0;
                long long npay = dp[r].pay + 1000LL * k;
                int ncoins = dp[r].coins + add;

                if (!ndp[nr].ok ||
                    ncoins > ndp[nr].coins ||
                    (ncoins == ndp[nr].coins && npay < ndp[nr].pay)) {
                    ndp[nr] = {ncoins, npay, true};
                }
            }
            dp.swap(ndp);
        }

        State ans = {-1, INF, false};
        for (int r = 0; r < 1000; ++r) {
            if (!dp[r].ok) continue;
            if (!ans.ok ||
                dp[r].coins > ans.coins ||
                (dp[r].coins == ans.coins && dp[r].pay < ans.pay)) {
                ans = dp[r];
            }
        }

        cout << ans.coins << ' ' << ans.pay << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}

